Tests/bench -- benchmarks
=========================

Two things live here, and neither is a lit suite (lit.local.cfg keeps the
whole directory out of collection).

  1. The builtin-library benchmarks: ops.toml, run.py, cpp/, rust/, kairo/.
     One row per library operation (string, slice and sort, vector, hash map
     and set, hasher, number text, iterator chains), measured in five
     columns and written up as one Markdown report. The rest of this file
     is about them.
  2. Whole-program benchmarks: the *.k / *.cc / *.rs files in this directory
     (nbody, gsort, spectral, ...), driven by bench-run.fish and
     bench-timing.py. Their header comments say how.


The five columns
----------------

    Kairo         kairo/<group>.k    kairo -O3, the installed library
    Kairo (new    kairo2/<group>.k   the compiler --kairo-new names (default:
    library)                         the most recently built debug or release
                                     kairo of this tree), -O3, with
                                     --builtins-dir Lib/builtin2 --std-dir
                                     Lib/std2 of this tree
    C++ (clang)   cpp/<group>.cpp    the toolchain's clang++, its libc++,
                                     the C library's malloc
    C++ (kcc)     cpp/<group>.cpp    kcc, the same libc++, linked with
                                     cpp/kcc_anchor.k
    Rust          rust/<group>.rs    rustc -C opt-level=3, std only

The two C++ columns are the same compiler, the same libc++ and the same
flags; they differ in the heap. A kcc link of C++ alone is clang's link and
keeps the C library's heap. A link that holds Kairo code carries the Kairo
runtime, whose mimalloc then serves malloc and operator new for the whole
program. cpp/kcc_anchor.k is that Kairo code. So clang against kcc shows
what the allocator is worth on each row. run.py checks both binaries with
llvm-nm (mi_malloc defined in the kcc one, absent from the clang one) and
stops if either is wrong.

The clang column is not `clang++ -stdlib=libc++` with the host's libc++: it
uses the sysroot kcc links with (run.py reads it from `kcc -###`), so the
library is the same in both.

A group with no kairo2 program yet leaves that column `nan`. A debug build
of the compiler is slower to compile; the code it emits goes through the
same clang at -O3. The report's header names the binary used.

Every compiler gets -O3 and the same target CPU: the name clang resolves
-march=native to (override with --cpu-name).


Running
-------

    python3 run.py                         everything
    python3 run.py --groups string,vector  some groups
    python3 run.py --rows 'map\.lookup'    rows whose id matches a regex
    python3 run.py --columns clang,rust    some columns
    python3 run.py --quick                 a smoke pass: 20 ms per cell, once,
                                           no counters
    python3 run.py --quiet-machine         for numbers to keep: 7 processes
                                           per cell in place of 3, and the
                                           report drops its noise warning

It needs python 3.11 or later, taskset, rustc, and the installed toolchain:
the first build/x86_64-linux-musl/release/bin at or above this directory,
or --toolchain / $KAIRO_TOOLCHAIN (--kairo, --kcc, --clang and --rustc name
single tools). perf is optional: where `perf stat` may count user-mode
events, a second pass reports instructions and cycles per operation; where
it may not, the report says so and has times only.

Output goes to out/ (--out): bin/<group>.<column>, results.json (every
number, with each process's time) and report.md (first a summary: one line per operation and size with
every column and the name of the fastest; then one table per group, the
fastest cell among all columns in bold, Kairo against the fastest baseline
as a ratio, and the new library against Rust and against the fastest
baseline).

One program can be run by hand:

    out/bin/string.clang list                    the cells it holds
    out/bin/string.clang string.find 4096        one cell
    out/bin/string.clang string.find             every size of a row
    out/bin/string.clang all --min-ms=50         everything, quicker
    env BENCH_ROW=string.eq BENCH_SIZE=8 out/bin/string.kairo

A Kairo main takes no arguments, so a Kairo program reads BENCH_ROW,
BENCH_SIZE, BENCH_MIN_MS and BENCH_REPS; the others accept either form.

How long: 13 minutes for everything with counters (measured once, on a
busy 24-thread machine), of which the builds are about two. The map group
is the largest part (its 10^7-entry tables are built once per process).
--quiet-machine runs seven processes per cell in place of three, so expect
about twice that; --quick is the builds plus a minute or two.

    python3 run.py --report-only           write report.md again from the
                                           results.json already in out/


The protocol
------------

cpp/bench.h is the reference; rust/bench.rs is the same thing in Rust, and
the Kairo programs call bench.h through `ffi "c++" import`.

  - A row is a loop of repetitions. Each repetition does its untimed setup,
    then the timed workload between bench_tic and bench_toc. The first
    repetition warms up and is dropped. Repetitions continue until 200 ms
    have been timed and at least three were. The row's time is the FASTEST
    repetition divided by the operations in a repetition. ops.toml says
    what one operation is.
  - Each program prints `id,size,ns_per_op,iterations` on stdout
    (iterations = timed repetitions; `nan,0` for a row it cannot run) and
    `check,id,size,checksum,ops_per_rep` on stderr.
  - The checksum is what the workload computed. Inputs come from one
    xorshift64 generator with one seed, written out in each language, so
    every column must print the same checksum for a cell. run.py compares
    them, marks a disagreeing row in the report and exits nonzero.
  - Results and inputs pass through an optimizer barrier (bench_sink,
    bench_sink_ptr, bench_opaque; std::hint::black_box in Rust), so a
    workload cannot be folded away or hoisted out of its loop.
  - What is timed is the same in every language. Destroying what a
    repetition built happens in the next repetition's setup, untimed,
    unless the row is about destruction (create + destroy, collect).
    A generator the timed code draws from is reseeded in the setup.
  - run.py runs each cell as its own process pinned to one CPU with
    taskset, the columns interleaved, and keeps the fastest process.
  - Counters: `perf stat` counts a whole process, so run.py runs a cell
    with K and with 2K repetitions and divides the difference by K
    repetitions' operations. Start-up, input generation and the warm-up
    cancel; a repetition's untimed setup does not, so on rows with a large
    setup (the sorts copy their input) the counters include it.

Fairness notes that are easy to miss:

  - `_fast` rows. The standard hashers are not comparable: libc++'s
    std::hash of an integer is the identity, Rust's default is SipHash-1-3
    with random keys. So the map and hasher rows come twice: with each
    library's default, and (`_fast`) with one fast hasher written out
    identically in bench.h and bench.rs. Kairo has one hasher; its programs
    answer both ids with the same code.
  - std::sort pairs with sort_unstable, std::stable_sort with sort.
  - String sizes sit on both sides of libc++'s 22-byte inline buffer
    (8 and 32 bytes); a Rust String is always on the heap.
  - C++20 has no enumerate, and a transform_view is an input range to
    vector's iterator-pair constructor; ops.toml names the C++20 spelling
    used in each such row.


Adding a row
------------

  1. ops.toml: add a [[row]] with a new id (`<group>.<name>`), its sizes,
     what one operation is and what is timed.
  2. cpp/<group>.cpp, rust/<group>.rs, kairo/<group>.k: write the row in
     each, in the shape of its neighbours, and add the
     `if bench_want("<id>", <size>)` line to main for every size. A
     language that cannot run it says so: `bench_skip()` under the
     bench_want (C++), `b.skip()` (Rust), `bench_todo("<id>", <size>)`
     (Kairo). run.py reports a program whose cells differ from ops.toml.
  3. Make the checksum depend on the result and on nothing else (not on
     how many repetitions ran), and compute it the same way in each
     language.
  4. `python3 run.py --quick --rows '<id>'` must finish without a checksum
     complaint.

A new group is a [[group]] entry in ops.toml and one file per language.


The Kairo column
----------------

kairo/*.k is written against the library the installed compiler ships
(ops.toml, `[kairo] library`, is printed in the report). Rows that library
has no operation for print `nan` and stay empty: every slice row, find,
swap_remove, the text-to-number rows and the iterator chains. To move a
program to another library, rewrite the bodies with that library's calls
(the `kairo` field of each row names the intended one), turn each
bench_todo into a real row, and update `[kairo] library`. The ids, sizes,
operation counts and checksums stay as they are; a wrong port shows up as a
checksum disagreement.
